duUtjM77SVvprVGMiaHG-cav_At0hH7CsjRYzg5XIUgtLwO3rBh7xULwI-ddjiuDbgzWUn067s5CA8sOg_QQx1MiZj__hR8uZ82KvqmxSOryIv0dIq2kzYCgTgLTwL15s-IWqMMGGdl0-b0v1Vx5h70PoC3j6JfxcaCm6j2L5rwim1sovnlDLfxEGOWgrRXrEaAjauzvVSDJGbLaBe_h8UIFZHOXKsIf0KV8hjDBrsUpupuwvBXW29TIbA_PRMyRvituYfmsqJTwlgPHxNvkHeswE18TlfQKApcNoSLlYTzW2RjZfs4PXfCWUwUhUCOWSvL5-2ijxdNRNonk6QeuBR668H2zosgyYfbvbFjdm9r1cInpM91_1W1UlNfWj1BgdZi0FWrtj1v9aJxbbKIXYc4XCM55-D7gvk0z4B_jY6iZOO-1ceu7nSM_tA0VYc-Y3cgkgLMIPggAPW08-zC72c4DyhU2CnVvrMvOqQshHlcygmhQxRcSlnRfELinvrW8IJiRejiz3xyI12_SUhQEOnRmHZxYt325OYpQsfU7gv-pEkbrwhcw0u43Sqie_AXfwvoR9eVKevyP-3WGNFIv8qp3RUn1eN4PmVoJuETaClJdrgtAG85T3LX_QHUZHHHblRNtLxdkg0wYU0T2JYIhuaDjKnIcC3wtgzQx-PRYkusjm8eBQm2TAmEdWTDgcIzvQ3CNnffyM6N6KuCj9S0Gpql-KDnnXkmMxeBeVYiyvHDZys2eqgbS8zXiHgJxafAIWI0X8PeRQK8yOXLlS62G-jsFVgwbsVhMyWhRk1FcaBRLbZzB0pjqw9am__ub-Pmq_VipqM9qz03FCDsPfch5AWOVxGrD9fTmhrfyQkWXl-Hc6EoBw1v7TfgFLyGZ8oXc5ZiI="},{"type":"function_call","id":"fc_09e7677d2c9d8e14016adb2f586ed88191acbf7fd67c6321b7","call_id":"call_fd9ef0044dda41f6887749598590bab7","name":"submit_artifact","arguments":"{\"artifact_json\":\"{\\\"status\\\":\\\"COMPLETED\\\",\\\"target\\\":\\\"For every prime p, field K of characteristic p, integer lambda with 0 <= lambda < p, and arbitrary admissible lattice M in V(lambda), M tensor_Z K is nonzero and irreducible under L(K).\\\",\\\"obstacle_assessment\\\":{\\\"classification\\\":\\\"ROUTINE_COMPLETION_NOT_SUBSTANTIVE_GAP\\\",\\\"reasoning\\\":\\\"The reported issue is routine: matrix-unit decomposition implies the coordinate module J is finitely generated, nonzero, and rank one; an elementary argument shows every such module over Z_(p) is free of rank one. Its generator yields a standard reduced basis and action. No unsupported implication remains, and arbitrary M and all stated degeneracies are preserved.\\\"},\\\"complete_proof\\\":\\\"Fix p,K,lambda,M as in the hypotheses, and let n=lambda+1 and R=Z_(p)={a/b in Q : p does not divide b}. Let v be a nonzero highest-weight vector in V(lambda), and set e_i=y^i v/i! for 0<=i<=lambda. These form a C-basis. Put X_r=x^r/r! and Y_r=y^r/r!. Repeated use of [h,x]=2x, [h,y]=-2y, [x,y]=h, xv=0 and hv=lambda v gives X_r e_i=binom(lambda-i+r,r)e_(i-r), and Y_r e_i=binom(i+r,r)e_(i+r); a basis vector with index outside 0,...,lambda means zero. Indeed x e_i=(lambda-i+1)e_(i-1) and y e_i=(i+1)e_(i+1), and iterating these formulas then dividing by r! gives the displayed binomial coefficients. Thus the divided-power matrices are integral in this basis. Let M_R=R tensor_Z M, embedded in V(lambda), and let A_R be the R-algebra of operators generated by the divided powers. Admissibility implies every such operator, and hence every element of A_R, preserves M_R.\\n\\nFor 0<=a,b<=lambda let E_ab send e_b to e_a and all other basis vectors to zero. The formulas show Y_lambda X_lambda=E_lambda,lambda, X_(lambda-i)E_lambda,lambda=E_i,lambda, and E_lambda,lambda Y_(lambda-j)=binom(lambda,j)E_lambda,j. For the first identity X_lambda kills e_i for i<lambda and sends e_lambda to e_0, while Y_lambda sends e_0 to e_lambda. Since lambda<p, binom(lambda,j) is a unit in R: its numerator and denominator factorials have no factor p. Therefore E_lambda,j belongs to A_R, and E_i,j=E_i,lambda E_lambda,j also belongs to A_R. For lambda=0 the sole matrix unit is the identity and these assertions still hold. Consequently all E_i,j preserve M_R.\\n\\nDefine J_i={c in C : c e_i belongs to M_R}. M_R is free of rank n and thus finitely generated over R. The image E_ii(M_R)=J_i e_i is finitely generated, so J_i is finitely generated. It is nonzero: M_R spans V(lambda) over C because a Z-basis of M is a C-basis by hypothesis, and the nonzero projection E_ii applied to that span has image C e_i. If J_i were zero, E_ii would vanish on M_R and therefore on its C-span, a contradiction. If c belongs to J_j, E_ij(c e_j)=c e_i belongs to M_R, giving J_j subset J_i; using E_ji gives equality. Write J for their common value. For m in M_R every coordinate projection E_ii m lies in M_R and is therefore c_i e_i for c_i in J. Conversely, for every c in J, c e_i belongs to M_R for every i. Since the e_i are independent, these facts prove M_R=direct_sum_(i=0)^lambda J e_i.\\n\\nWe supply the module details. Let Q be the fraction field of R. Localizing that direct-sum equality gives Q tensor_R M_R=direct_sum_i (Q tensor_R J)e_i. The left side has Q-dimension n, since M_R is free of rank n. Thus n=n dim_Q(Q tensor_R J), and dim_Q(Q tensor_R J)=1. Also J is torsion-free, being an R-submodule of C. Choose a Q-linear identification Q tensor_R J with Q. The image I of J is a nonzero finitely generated R-submodule of Q. Clearing denominators of a finite generating set gives a nonzero finitely generated ideal dI of R for some nonzero d in R. Every nonzero q in Q is p^k u for an integer k and a unit u in R: write q=p^k a/b with p dividing neither a nor b. If B is a nonzero finitely generated ideal of R, take a finite set of nonzero generators and choose the smallest p-exponent k among them. Every generator lies in p^k R, and a generator with exponent k is p^k times a unit. Hence B=p^k R. Applied to dI, this proves I is principal. Therefore J is free of rank one over R; choose a generator a. We now have the R-basis a e_0,...,a e_lambda of M_R.\\n\\nThe actions on this basis follow from the formulas for r=1 and h e_i=(lambda-2i)e_i: x(ae_i)=(lambda-i+1)ae_(i-1), y(ae_i)=(i+1)ae_(i+1), and h(ae_i)=(lambda-2i)ae_i, with out-of-range terms zero. Reduction modulo p therefore gives the standard reduced module on the reduced basis vectors. Explicitly, M_R/pM_R identifies with M tensor_Z F_p: in any Z-basis of M both are vector spaces on the corresponding reduced basis, and the action matrices are reductions of the same integral matrices. The R-basis just obtained identifies these actions with the standard ones. Extending scalars along the prime-subfield inclusion F_p subset K identifies M tensor_Z K with that standard module over K, respecting x,y,h.\\n\\nFinally let W be a K-subspace of the standard reduced module invariant under x,y,h. For 0<=r<=lambda<p, r! is invertible modulo p, so X_r and Y_r are scalar multiples of x^r and y^r on this module. The matrix-unit identities above hold after reduction, since every binom(lambda,j) is nonzero modulo p. Thus every E_i,j is generated by compositions and scalar multiples of x and y, and preserves W. If W is nonzero, take w=sum_i c_i e_i in W with c_i nonzero for some i. For every j, E_ji w=c_i e_j lies in W; division by c_i shows e_j lies in W. Hence W contains the full basis and equals the whole module. Its dimension lambda+1 is positive. This proves irreducibility, including lambda=0, and uses no restriction p>2 or distinctness of h-weights.\\\",\\\"source_obligations\\\":\\\"No literature theorem is imported. The highest-weight basis and divided-power formulas are derived from the stated sl2 relations and highest-weight equations by iteration; matrix-unit identities are explicit calculations. Finite generation, localization, rank, the principal-ideal argument for Z_(p), reduction, and scalar extension are shown in the proof. The starting facts that V(lambda) has the stated highest-weight vector and basis dimension, M is free of rank lambda+1 and spans V(lambda), and admissibility means U_Z M is contained in M come from the supplied source-context and hypotheses; none is a claim about modular irreducibility.\\\",\\\"edge_cases\\\":\\\"lambda=0 gives a one-dimensional nonzero module with sole matrix unit E_00, hence irreducible. For p=2 the range permits only lambda=0 or 1; the proof uses no division by 2 and the binomial coefficients required are units. Arbitrary admissible M is treated, not just a selected lattice. Every field K of characteristic p is covered by extension from F_p.\\\"}\"}","status":"completed"}],"error":null,"incomplete_details":null,"tools":[{"type":"function","name":"search_mathlib","description":"在固定 mathlib 的 Lean 源码中搜索正则表达式，返回至多 100 行。","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"query":{"type":"string"}},"required":["query"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"read_mathlib","description":"读取固定 mathlib 中一个 Mathlib/...lean 文件的指定行。","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"path":{"type":"string"},"start_line":{"type":"integer"},"line_count":{"type":"integer"}},"required":["path","start_line","line_count"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"read_task_input","description":"Read an explicitly frozen task document by ID; no other local files are accessible.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"id":{"type":"string"},"start":{"type":"integer"},"end":{"type":"integer"}},"required":["id","start","end"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"read_yui","description":"Read declared Yui rules/templates; no tests, historical runs or personal files.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"path":{"type":"string"},"start":{"type":"integer"},"end":{"type":"integer"}},"required":["path","start","end"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"check_artifact","description":"Check a JSON artifact against the explicit stage contract; this is not automatic mathematical certification.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"artifact_json":{"type":"string"}},"required":["artifact_json"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"submit_artifact","description":"Submit a JSON artifact after its stage checks.","parameters":{"type":"object","properties":{"artifact_json":{"type":"string"}},"required":["artifact_json"],"additionalProperties":false},"output_schema":null,"strict":false},{"type":"function","name":"report_obstacle","description":"Stop this stage honestly on a substantive or suspected proof gap, un